  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A large block of wood of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>99</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math> is hanging from two long massless cords. A bullet of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> is fired into the block and gets embedded in it. The (block <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo></math>&nbsp;bullet) then swing upwards, their center of mass rising a vertical distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> before the (block <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo></math> bullet) pendulum comes momentarily to rest at the end of its arc. The speed of the bullet just before the collision is:&nbsp;(Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>)</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0056_ff54ee1cfd4a.png\" style=\"width: 236px; height: 208px;\" /></p>"
  },
